Below is one possible investigation path – from identifying Abakan’s airport code to calculating the great-circle (Haversine) distance for every route that starts or ends there, then returning only the longest one.

```sql
-- Description: 1) Find airport(s) whose English city name contains ‘Abakan’.
SELECT DISTINCT
       airport_code,
       json_extract(city,'$.en') AS city_en
FROM   airports_data
WHERE  json_extract(city,'$.en') LIKE '%Abakan%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- Description: 2) (Optional) Make sure we did not miss the Russian name.
SELECT DISTINCT
       airport_code,
       json_extract(city,'$.ru') AS city_ru
FROM   airports_data
WHERE  json_extract(city,'$.ru') LIKE '%Абакан%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- Description: 3) List flights that DEPART from Abakan (code found above, e.g. 'ABA').
SELECT DISTINCT
       flight_no,
       departure_airport,
       arrival_airport
FROM   flights
WHERE  departure_airport = 'ABA'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- Description: 4) List flights that ARRIVE in Abakan.
SELECT DISTINCT
       flight_no,
       departure_airport,
       arrival_airport
FROM   flights
WHERE  arrival_airport = 'ABA'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- Description: 5) All unique “other” airports paired with Abakan on any route.
SELECT DISTINCT
       CASE WHEN departure_airport = 'ABA'
            THEN arrival_airport
            ELSE departure_airport
       END AS other_airport
FROM   flights
WHERE  departure_airport = 'ABA' OR arrival_airport = 'ABA'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- Description: 6) Show Abakan’s coordinates (lon, lat) once for reference.
SELECT airport_code,
       coordinates
FROM   airports_data
WHERE  airport_code = 'ABA';
```

```sql
-- Description: 7) Join every Abakan flight with the “other” airport and expose both coordinates.
SELECT  f.flight_id,
        f.departure_airport,
        f.arrival_airport,
        ad1.coordinates AS aba_coords,
        ad2.coordinates AS other_coords
FROM    flights        AS f
JOIN    airports_data  AS ad1 ON ad1.airport_code = 'ABA'
JOIN    airports_data  AS ad2 ON ad2.airport_code =
        CASE WHEN f.departure_airport = 'ABA'
             THEN f.arrival_airport
             ELSE f.departure_airport END
WHERE   f.departure_airport = 'ABA' OR f.arrival_airport = 'ABA'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- Description: 8) Compute great-circle distance (in km) for every Abakan route.
-- coordinates[0] = longitude, coordinates[1] = latitude when using the Postgres “point” type.
SELECT DISTINCT
        ad2.airport_code                                       AS other_airport,
        ROUND( 6371 * ACOS(
              COS(RADIANS( ad1.coordinates[1] )) *
              COS(RADIANS( ad2.coordinates[1] )) *
              COS(RADIANS( ad2.coordinates[0] ) -
                  RADIANS( ad1.coordinates[0] )) +
              SIN(RADIANS( ad1.coordinates[1] )) *
              SIN(RADIANS( ad2.coordinates[1] ))
        ) , 1)                                                AS distance_km
FROM    airports_data  AS ad1
JOIN    flights        AS f  ON (f.departure_airport = ad1.airport_code
                              OR f.arrival_airport   = ad1.airport_code)
JOIN    airports_data  AS ad2 ON ad2.airport_code =
        CASE WHEN f.departure_airport = ad1.airport_code
             THEN f.arrival_airport
             ELSE f.departure_airport END
WHERE   ad1.airport_code = 'ABA';
```

```sql
-- Description: 9) Return only the longest Abakan route (final answer).
SELECT  MAX(distance_km) AS longest_abakan_route_km
FROM  (
        SELECT DISTINCT
               ROUND( 6371 * ACOS(
                     COS(RADIANS( ad1.coordinates[1] )) *
                     COS(RADIANS( ad2.coordinates[1] )) *
                     COS(RADIANS( ad2.coordinates[0] )
                         - RADIANS( ad1.coordinates[0] )) +
                     SIN(RADIANS( ad1.coordinates[1] )) *
                     SIN(RADIANS( ad2.coordinates[1] ))
               ), 1) AS distance_km
        FROM    airports_data AS ad1
        JOIN    flights       AS f  ON (f.departure_airport = ad1.airport_code
                                     OR f.arrival_airport   = ad1.airport_code)
        JOIN    airports_data AS ad2 ON ad2.airport_code =
               CASE WHEN f.departure_airport = ad1.airport_code
                    THEN f.arrival_airport
                    ELSE f.departure_airport END
        WHERE   ad1.airport_code = 'ABA'
      ) sub;
```

Running the last query returns one number – the great-circle length (in kilometres) of the farthest route that either departs from, or arrives at, Abakan.
